Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Lumen**, a floating beacon drone whose front emitter
tilts while its two rings counter-rotate around a glowing core, as a 40x56x40
opaque-voxel model using only the voxel-anim tool.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already
on your `PATH`. It is the sole channel: you build the model by calling it **one
operation at a time**, and the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to
`parts/<part>.actions.json` — together with the rig you define are the
**authoritative output that is scored**, not the voxels on disk. Anything produced
another way (writing files directly, editing a preview by hand) is discarded and
flagged; only the recorded operations and the rig count.

Every voxel operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is
sculpted **separately** in its own preview and log. The volume starts **empty**.
Voxels are **opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. After each call
the binary re-renders that part's isometric preview to `parts/<part>.png` and
appends the operation to that part's log. Blank starting previews, empty per-part
logs, and a pre-seeded `rig.json` holding the required parts and joints are already
in place at `{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim fill-box --part core --x0 10 --y0 24 --z0 10 --x1 29 --y1 44 --z1 29 --color "#c69a4b"
```

Run `voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig and animation
subcommands (defining parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring
animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read `parts/<part>.png` after your calls** to see that
part's progress, **and read the assembled-scene previews under `scene/`
(`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the
whole drone composed from all your parts, re-rendered after every operation. The
scene views are how you confirm the parts actually fit together: that the rings
sit out at the core's sides and the emitter meets the core face. Because each part
is sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates, position each part where it belongs
on the finished drone; the scene simply composes them there.

